#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161675 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1af00587-e4c1-5199-828a-357709e69785" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Distribution of the Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "cc2a7119-cb80-575b-9e90-d20ea283de5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10085" :question/problem "Given that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{28} \\sim N(8, 14^2)$ is a random sample of size $n = 28$ from a normal population, what is the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 28, \\qquad\n\\mu = 8, \\qquad\n\\sigma = 14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(8,\\frac{{14}^{2}}{28}) \\\\  & ∼N(8,7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161676 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0643d82d-39a0-544e-b4b1-f178d14e4e03" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "82ae81dd-526e-50de-850c-f1623aae21fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10086" :question/problem "Given that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots,X_{100} \\sim N(-4, 20^2)$ is a random sample of size $100$ from a normal population, calculate $P(-4.3 \\lt \\overline{X} \\lt -3.9).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4443 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4404 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4364 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5160 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5199 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5239 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5596 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5636 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-4,\\frac{{20}^{2}}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(-4,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(-4.3 \\lt \\overline{X} \\lt -3.9).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-4.3<\\overset{―}{X}<-3.9) & =P(\\frac{-4.3-(-4)}{\\sqrt{4}}<Z<\\frac{-3.9-(-4)}{\\sqrt{4}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.15<Z<0.05) \\\\  & =Φ(0.05)-Φ(-0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.05)=0.5199, \\\\ Φ(-0.15)=0.4404.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.15<Z<0.05) & =Φ(0.05)-Φ(-0.15) \\\\  & =0.5199-0.4404 \\\\  & =0.0795.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161677 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc5b49c9-5fd8-574b-aed6-4cec9421d3d1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sample Mean in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "8cbddf16-bfab-539b-bce1-5ccb59dc4313" :question/math-academy-id "e-10087" :question/problem "The diastolic blood pressure of adult men in a given population is normally distributed with a mean of $75\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $10\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Suppose $10$ men are selected at random. What is the probability that the mean diastolic blood pressure for this sample will be less than $70\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. * \n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0606 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0594 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0582 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0571 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0559 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0735 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0721 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0708 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0694 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0681 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0869 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0853 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0838 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0823 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\nX_i\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu=75,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma = 10,$ and that the sample size $n=10.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(75,\\frac{{10}^{2}}{10}) \\\\  & ∼N(75,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P( \\overline{X} \\lt 70).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-75}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.58) & =0.0571.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<70) & =Φ(-1.58) \\\\  & =0.0571.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1af00587-e4c1-5199-828a-357709e69785" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Distribution of the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04699e55-d487-568d-acba-2efd6750322d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(6,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421562} {:answer/id #uuid "7bce8468-700e-5380-9202-63982bf2eba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(12,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421582} {:answer/id #uuid "91f28a63-d7ec-5388-a9e1-43440f951e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(12,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421585} {:answer/id #uuid "a36e3c8c-89ea-523b-b7d5-b53a87c7e654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421589} {:answer/id #uuid "a922ce22-659c-5162-8aee-d79caddff8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(12,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a922ce22-659c-5162-8aee-d79caddff8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(12,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421592} :answer-field/id #uuid "76152c14-a598-5713-8008-19c33ac89a67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc9af1e1-2430-571a-97fc-932c8c2c6f9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143263" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}∼N(12,{6}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $n=18$ from a normal population, what is the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=18,\\,\\mu =12,\\,\\sigma =6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(12,\\frac{{6}^{2}}{18}) \\\\  & ∼N(12,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1af00587-e4c1-5199-828a-357709e69785" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Distribution of the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fcbf42-2fbd-5ed9-8cf4-cd718dee0134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∼N(25,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421564} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9c6366-7861-5a5c-b561-88aad00f2e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∼N(-5,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421572} {:answer/id #uuid "45835fb9-b85b-57bc-a1aa-a6f244edcf59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∼N(25,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421573} {:answer/id #uuid "45f5830b-b468-5a44-91a9-4f53e0fba1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∼N(-5,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421574} {:answer/id #uuid "47c08bb8-c4ad-54a7-b9cc-ac1ff56ecded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∼N(-5,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e9c6366-7861-5a5c-b561-88aad00f2e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∼N(-5,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421572} :answer-field/id #uuid "e236b4e9-4a3f-571b-9649-b62022424cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :ques